entic Evolution.\n\nLiu Yang, Zeyu Nie, Andrew Liu, Felix Zou, Deniz Altinbüken, Amir Yazdanbakhsh, Quanquan C. Liu:\nParEVO: Synthesizing Code for Irregular Data: High-Performance Parallelism through Agentic Evolution. CoRR abs/2603.02510 (2026)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i32]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2605-01960\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nParEVO: Synthesizing Code for Irregular Data: High-Performance Parallelism through Agentic Evolution — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/parevo-synthesizing-code-for-irregular-data-high-performance-parallelism-through/wrk_1dcf9ae6c8b527940ca1af0de0386a72)\nciteturn32110search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # ParEVO: Synthesizing Code for Irregular Data: High-Performance Parallelism through Agentic Evolution ... Fundamentally, standard LLMs lack an internal \"work-span\" model to understand how to distribute tasks efficiently across multiple cores.To address this, researchers have introduced ParEVO, a novel framework that enables AI to synthesize high-performance parallel algorithms for irregular data. ... Data-Centric Synthesis: The foundation of ParEVO is the newly created Parlay-Instruct Corpus, a dataset comprising 13,820 verified parallel coding tasks.\n\n# ParEVO: Synthesizing Code for Irregular Data: High-Performance Parallelism through Agentic Evolution\n\nLiu Yang, Zeyu Nie, Andrew Liu, Ruomu Zou, Deniz Altınbüken, Amir Yazdanbakhsh, Quanquan C. Liu\n\nICML 2026 · OpenReview\n\nRead Paper Bookmark\n\nParallel programming is inherently challenging, but applying it to \"irregular\" data structures—such as sparse graphs, unbalanced trees, and non-uniform meshes—represents the true \"hard mode\" of high-performance computing. Unlike regular tasks with predictable workloads, like dense matrix multiplication, irregular algorithms feature memory access patterns and workloads that shift dynamically at runtime.\n\nCurrent Large Language Models (LLMs) fail catastrophically in this domain. Burdened by a \"sequential bias,\" they frequently generate thread-unsafe code riddled with race conditions and deadlocks, or they apply locks so heavily that the parallelized code runs slower than a single-threaded baseline. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHome (https://www.denizaltinbuken.com/home)\nciteturn32110search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago;   * Our paper ParEVO: Synthesizing Code for Irregular Data: High-Performance Parallelism through Agentic Evolution has been accepted to ICML'26.\n\nhello@denizaltinbuken.com\n\nNews\n\n  * Our paper ParEVO: Synthesizing Code for Irregular Data: High-Performance Parallelism through Agentic Evolution has been accepted to ICML'26.\n\n  * Our paper ACE: An AI-Driven Code Efficiency Optimizer for Warehouse Scale Computers has been accepted to OSDI'2 6.\n\n  * On the PC for OSDI'26, SOSP'26, Eurosys'26, ATC'26.\n\n  *  On the ATC Steering Committee (2026).\n\n  * Our paper Natural Language Outlines for Code: Literate Programming in the LLM Era has been accepted to FSE'25.\n\n  * Our paper Snowplow: Effective Kernel Fuzzing with a Learned White-box Test Mutator has been accepted to ASPLOS'25.\n\n  * On the PC for SOSP'25.\n\n  * Serving as a reviewer on ML for Systems @NeurIPS'24.\n\n  * Our workshop PACMI has been accepted to SOSP'24.\n\n  * Serving as reviewer on MLArchSys 2024 @ ISCA.\n"
